    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The Bible presents many men and women whom God raised up to lead His people at critical moments in history. In this first study of four great leaders — <span class="learn-keyword">Joseph</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">Moses</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">Joshua</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">Deborah</span> — we examine their early lives and how God called each of them into leadership.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Joseph — The Dreamer Who Became a Ruler</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Joseph was the eleventh son of Jacob and the first son of his beloved wife Rachel (Genesis 30:22-24). His father\'s open favouritism — shown through the famous <strong>coat of many colours</strong> — and Joseph\'s own dreams of future greatness stirred deep jealousy in his brothers (Genesis 37:3-11). Out of envy, his brothers sold him into slavery to Ishmaelite/Midianite traders, who took him to Egypt (Genesis 37:23-28). In Egypt, Joseph served in the house of Potiphar, was falsely accused and imprisoned, yet remained faithful to God throughout. His gift of interpreting dreams eventually brought him before Pharaoh, and he was made second-in-command over all Egypt (Genesis 41:39-41).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Moses — Called from the Burning Bush</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Moses was born to Hebrew parents of the tribe of Levi at a time when Pharaoh had ordered every Hebrew baby boy to be killed (Exodus 1:22). To save him, his mother hid him for three months, then placed him in a basket among the reeds of the River Nile, where he was found and adopted by Pharaoh\'s daughter (Exodus 2:1-10). Raised in the Egyptian palace, Moses later fled to Midian after killing an Egyptian who was beating a Hebrew slave. While shepherding in Midian, God called him at the burning bush that burned but was not consumed, commissioning him to deliver Israel from slavery in Egypt (Exodus 3:1-10).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Joshua — Trained Under Moses</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Joshua, son of Nun, from the tribe of Ephraim, served as Moses\' personal assistant. He was one of the twelve spies sent to explore Canaan and, along with Caleb, gave a faithful, courageous report that trusted in God\'s promise, unlike the other ten spies who spread fear (Numbers 13:1-2, 30; 14:6-9). Because of his faith, Joshua (with Caleb) was permitted to enter the Promised Land even though the rest of that generation died in the wilderness.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Deborah — Prophetess and Judge</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Deborah was a prophetess and the only female judge mentioned in the Book of Judges. She held court under the "Palm of Deborah" between Ramah and Bethel, where the Israelites came to her for judgment (Judges 4:4-5). At a time when Israel was oppressed by Jabin, king of Canaan, and his army commander Sisera, Deborah summoned Barak and, speaking under God\'s direction, commanded him to gather an army against Sisera (Judges 4:6-7).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Leader</th><th>Background</th><th>How God Called/Prepared Them</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Joseph</td><td>Favoured son of Jacob, sold into slavery</td><td>Prepared through suffering and faithfulness in Egypt</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Moses</td><td>Hebrew child raised in Pharaoh\'s palace</td><td>Called at the burning bush in Midian</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Joshua</td><td>Assistant to Moses, faithful spy</td><td>Trained under Moses; commissioned after his death</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deborah</td><td>Prophetess and judge of Israel</td><td>Held divine authority to judge and direct military action</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Examiners often ask for the specific location or circumstance of each leader\'s call — the burning bush for Moses, the "Palm of Deborah" for Deborah, and the report of the twelve spies for Joshua. Learn these details precisely.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Joseph, Moses, Joshua and Deborah each experienced hardship or preparation before God raised them into leadership. Their early lives teach that God often prepares leaders through humble or difficult beginnings before entrusting them with responsibility.\n</div>\n',
