    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Having studied the early lives and calls of Joseph, Moses, Joshua and Deborah, we now turn to their <span class="learn-keyword">achievements</span>, the <span class="learn-keyword">challenges</span> they faced, and the enduring <span class="learn-keyword">lessons</span> their leadership offers.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Joseph\'s Achievements and Challenges</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">As governor of Egypt, Joseph implemented a wise food-storage plan during seven years of plenty, saving Egypt and neighbouring nations from a severe seven-year famine (Genesis 41:46-57). He faced great personal challenges — betrayal by his brothers, false accusation by Potiphar\'s wife, and years of imprisonment — yet did not become bitter. When his brothers eventually came to Egypt for food, he forgave them, famously declaring, "You meant evil against me, but God meant it for good" (Genesis 50:20).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Moses\' Achievements and Challenges</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Moses confronted Pharaoh through the ten plagues, led Israel out of Egyptian bondage across the Red Sea, received the Ten Commandments at Mount Sinai, and guided the nation through forty years in the wilderness (Exodus 7-20). His challenges included Pharaoh\'s repeated refusals, the people\'s frequent complaints and rebellion (such as the golden calf incident, Exodus 32), and his own moments of frustration, including striking the rock in anger, which cost him entry into Canaan (Numbers 20:7-12).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Joshua\'s Achievements and Challenges</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">After Moses\' death, Joshua led Israel across the River Jordan and conquered the Promised Land, beginning with the miraculous fall of Jericho (Joshua 6). He faced the challenge of leading a nation still learning obedience, including the defeat at Ai caused by Achan\'s sin (Joshua 7), and the responsibility of dividing the conquered land among the twelve tribes (Joshua 13-21).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Deborah\'s Achievements and Challenges</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Deborah, together with Barak, led Israel to a decisive victory over Sisera\'s army, which was destroyed near the River Kishon (Judges 4:15-16). Sisera himself was later killed by Jael. Deborah\'s challenge was rallying a discouraged, oppressed nation and a hesitant military commander into confident action. Her victory is celebrated in the Song of Deborah (Judges 5), one of the oldest poems in the Bible.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Leader</th><th>Key Achievement</th><th>Major Challenge</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Joseph</td><td>Saved Egypt and his family from famine</td><td>Betrayal, false accusation, imprisonment</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Moses</td><td>Delivered Israel from Egypt; received the Law</td><td>Pharaoh\'s resistance; the people\'s rebellion</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Joshua</td><td>Conquered Canaan; divided the land</td><td>Defeat at Ai; leading a young, untested nation</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deborah</td><td>Defeated Sisera\'s army with Barak</td><td>Rallying a fearful, oppressed people</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to quote or paraphrase Joseph\'s famous forgiveness statement in Genesis 50:20, and to explain the causes of Israel\'s setbacks under Moses and Joshua (golden calf; Achan\'s sin at Ai).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Each leader achieved great things for Israel but also faced serious setbacks. Their responses — forgiveness, perseverance, discipline and courage — model godly leadership under pressure.\n</div>\n',
