    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  In this final study of the four leaders, we consider the <span class="learn-keyword">relevance</span> of Joseph, Moses, Joshua and Deborah\'s leadership examples to <span class="learn-keyword">contemporary Christian life</span>. Their stories are not merely historical accounts; they offer timeless principles for leadership, faith and character today.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Joseph\'s Relevance Today</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Joseph teaches Christians to remain faithful and maintain integrity even when suffering unjustly, to resist temptation regardless of the cost, and to forgive those who wrong us. His example encourages believers to trust that God can work all things — even painful experiences — together for good (Romans 8:28, echoing Genesis 50:20).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Moses\' Relevance Today</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Moses shows that God can use ordinary, even reluctant, people for extraordinary purposes, and that leaders must remain humble and obedient to God\'s instructions even after long service. His life reminds Christian leaders today of the importance of patience with difficult people and the seriousness of obeying God precisely, not just approximately.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Joshua\'s Relevance Today</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Joshua\'s courage — captured in God\'s charge to him, "Be strong and of good courage... for the Lord your God is with you wherever you go" (Joshua 1:9) — encourages Christians to face new responsibilities boldly, trusting in God\'s presence. His careful, step-by-step obedience in conquering Canaan models disciplined, faithful leadership.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Deborah\'s Relevance Today</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Deborah demonstrates that God calls and equips both men and women for leadership and that spiritual discernment (prophecy) can guide practical, even military, decisions. Her partnership with Barak also shows the value of encouraging and supporting others into their own calling rather than seeking sole credit.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Leader</th><th>Timeless Principle for Christians Today</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Joseph</td><td>Integrity under pressure; forgiveness; trust in God\'s providence</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Moses</td><td>Obedience to God even in leadership fatigue; humility despite great responsibility</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Joshua</td><td>Courage in new responsibility; careful, faithful obedience</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Deborah</td><td>God uses willing, faithful people regardless of gender; encouraging others in their calling</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> When asked for "relevance to contemporary Christian life," always connect the Old Testament example to a specific present-day application — for example, workplace integrity (Joseph), obeying God precisely (Moses), courage in new roles (Joshua), or gender-inclusive leadership (Deborah).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The examples of Joseph, Moses, Joshua and Deborah remain relevant today as models of integrity, obedience, courage, and faithful service — qualities every Christian leader is called to embody.\n</div>\n',
