    term: 'First Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The account of <span class="learn-keyword">Eli</span> and his sons, Hophni and Phinehas, is a sobering biblical lesson on <span class="learn-keyword">parental responsibility</span>. It shows the serious consequences that can follow when parents fail to correct and discipline their children.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Who Was Eli?</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Eli was the high priest of Israel and judge at Shiloh, where the Ark of the Covenant and tabernacle were kept. His sons, Hophni and Phinehas, served as priests under him but were described as "corrupt" and had no regard for the Lord (1 Samuel 2:12).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Sins of Hophni and Phinehas</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Hophni and Phinehas abused their priestly office: they took the best portions of sacrificial meat for themselves by force before it was properly offered to God (1 Samuel 2:12-17), and they committed sexual immorality with women who served at the entrance of the tabernacle (1 Samuel 2:22).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Eli\'s Failure as a Parent</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Though Eli verbally rebuked his sons for their wickedness (1 Samuel 2:23-25), he failed to remove them from their priestly duties or take decisive corrective action. God later told Eli, through both a man of God and the boy Samuel, that Eli honoured his sons above God because he did not restrain them despite knowing their sin (1 Samuel 2:29; 3:13).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Consequences</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Because of Eli\'s failure to discipline his sons, God pronounced judgment on his household: both Hophni and Phinehas would die on the same day, the priesthood would be taken from Eli\'s family line, and Israel would suffer defeat with the Ark of the Covenant captured by the Philistines (1 Samuel 2:34; 4:10-11, 17-18). Eli himself died from shock upon hearing the news.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Aspect</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Eli\'s role</td><td>High priest and judge of Israel at Shiloh</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sons\' sins</td><td>Stealing sacrificial meat; sexual immorality; contempt for God\'s offerings</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Eli\'s failure</td><td>Rebuked but did not decisively restrain or remove them</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Consequence</td><td>Death of both sons in one day; loss of priesthood from his line; Israel\'s defeat</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A common exam question asks what specific sins Hophni and Phinehas committed — remember both the abuse of sacrificial meat and their immorality. Also know that it was young Samuel who delivered part of God\'s judgment message to Eli (1 Samuel 3:11-14).</strong>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Eli\'s story teaches that parents bear real responsibility to correct and restrain their children\'s wrongdoing. Mere verbal rebuke without decisive action is insufficient, and neglect of parental duty carries serious spiritual and practical consequences.\n</div>\n',
