    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The friendship of <span class="learn-keyword">David and Jonathan</span> is one of the most celebrated examples of loyal friendship in the Bible. This topic examines their relationship as a model for understanding true friendship from a Christian perspective.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Beginning of David and Jonathan\'s Friendship</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">After David killed Goliath, Jonathan, son of King Saul, formed an immediate deep bond with David: "the soul of Jonathan was knit to the soul of David, and Jonathan loved him as his own soul" (1 Samuel 18:1). Jonathan made a covenant with David and gave him his robe, armour, sword, bow and belt, symbolically sharing his royal status and honour with David (1 Samuel 18:3-4).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Jonathan\'s Loyalty Despite Personal Cost</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">As King Saul grew jealous and sought to kill David, Jonathan repeatedly protected his friend, warning him of danger, pleading with his father on David\'s behalf, and helping him escape (1 Samuel 19:1-7; 20:1-42). Jonathan did this even though David\'s rise threatened his own claim to the throne, showing that Jonathan valued friendship and God\'s will above personal ambition.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Covenant Between David and Jonathan</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">David and Jonathan renewed their covenant of friendship multiple times, asking God to be witness between them and their descendants forever (1 Samuel 20:14-17, 42). After Jonathan\'s death in battle, David honoured this covenant by showing kindness to Jonathan\'s disabled son, Mephibosheth, restoring his grandfather\'s land and giving him a permanent place at David\'s table (2 Samuel 9:1-13).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Christian Perspective on Loyalty and Sacrifice</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">David and Jonathan\'s friendship illustrates biblical loyalty (Hebrew: <em>hesed</em>, covenant faithfulness), self-sacrifice, and putting another\'s wellbeing before personal ambition — qualities Jesus later commended: "Greater love has no one than this, that someone lay down his life for his friends" (John 15:13).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Quality Shown</th><th>Biblical Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Immediate deep bond</td><td>Jonathan\'s soul "knit" to David\'s after Goliath (1 Samuel 18:1)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Selfless generosity</td><td>Jonathan gave David his robe, armour, sword, bow, belt (1 Samuel 18:4)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Loyalty despite personal cost</td><td>Jonathan protected David despite Saul\'s jealousy and his own claim to the throne</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Covenant faithfulness beyond death</td><td>David\'s kindness to Mephibosheth (2 Samuel 9)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember the specific items Jonathan gave David (robe, armour, sword, bow, belt) and the name of Jonathan\'s son whom David later honoured (Mephibosheth) — these details are often tested directly.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> David and Jonathan\'s friendship models covenant loyalty, selfless generosity, and sacrifice — qualities that reflect the New Testament ideal of laying down one\'s life for a friend.\n</div>\n',
