    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  This topic examines biblical characters who exemplify <span class="learn-keyword">trust in God</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">submission</span> to His will, even in the face of uncertainty, hardship, or personal cost — namely <span class="learn-keyword">Abraham</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">Job</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">Jesus</span>.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Abraham\'s Trust in God</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Abraham left his home in Ur for an unknown land simply because God commanded him to (Genesis 12:1-4; Hebrews 11:8). His greatest test of trust came when God commanded him to offer his only promised son, Isaac, as a sacrifice. Abraham obeyed, trusting that God would provide, and was stopped by an angel at the last moment, with a ram provided as a substitute (Genesis 22:1-14).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Job\'s Trust Amid Suffering</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Job, a righteous and blameless man, lost his wealth, children, and health in swift succession through calamities permitted by God. Despite intense suffering and the pressure of his wife and friends to curse God, Job maintained his trust, declaring, "Though he slay me, yet will I trust in him" (Job 13:15) and, "The Lord gave, and the Lord has taken away; blessed be the name of the Lord" (Job 1:21).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Jesus\' Submission to God\'s Will</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">In the Garden of Gethsemane, facing the agony of the coming crucifixion, Jesus prayed, "Father, if you are willing, take this cup from me; yet not my will, but yours be done" (Luke 22:42). His complete submission to the Father\'s will, even unto death, models the ultimate example of trust and obedience.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Importance of Submission to God\'s Will</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">These examples show that trust in God does not mean the absence of hardship, fear, or difficult questions, but a settled confidence that God\'s will and purposes are ultimately good and trustworthy, even when circumstances are painful or unclear.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Test of Trust</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Abraham</td><td>Command to offer Isaac as a sacrifice</td><td>Obeyed, trusting God would provide</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Job</td><td>Loss of wealth, children, and health</td><td>Maintained trust despite intense suffering</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Jesus</td><td>The agony of the cross</td><td>Submitted to the Father\'s will in Gethsemane</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to quote key trust statements exactly: Abraham\'s faith journey (Hebrews 11:8), Job\'s declaration (Job 13:15; 1:21), and Jesus\' Gethsemane prayer (Luke 22:42).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Abraham, Job, and Jesus each modelled deep trust in and submission to God\'s will, even amid uncertainty, suffering, or fear — teaching that true faith holds firm regardless of circumstances.\n</div>\n',
