    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Every day, Christians face choices — some small, some life-changing. This topic explores the <span class="learn-keyword">factors</span> that influence decision-making and <span class="learn-keyword">biblical guidelines</span> for making right decisions that honour God.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Factors That Influence Decision-Making</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Decisions are shaped by many factors: personal desires and emotions, peer or family pressure, cultural and societal expectations, available information, past experiences, and — for the Christian — the desire to honour God. Recognizing these influences helps believers evaluate whether a decision is truly guided by godly wisdom or merely by pressure or emotion.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Biblical Guidelines for Right Decision-Making</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Scripture offers clear guidance for believers: seeking God\'s will through prayer (Philippians 4:6-7), relying on Scripture as a guide ("Your word is a lamp to my feet and a light to my path," Psalm 119:105), seeking godly counsel (Proverbs 15:22), trusting God rather than one\'s own limited understanding (Proverbs 3:5-6), and considering whether a decision aligns with God\'s character and commands.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Biblical Examples of Decision-Making</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Solomon\'s request for wisdom rather than wealth or long life (1 Kings 3:5-14) shows a decision aligned with godly priorities. By contrast, Lot\'s choice of the well-watered plain of Sodom, based purely on material advantage without regard for spiritual danger, led to disaster (Genesis 13:10-13; 19:1-29).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Practical Steps for Christian Decision-Making</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">A Christian approach to decisions typically involves: prayerful reflection, checking Scripture for relevant principles, seeking counsel from mature believers, considering long-term consequences (spiritual and practical), and being willing to act in obedience once God\'s direction becomes clear.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Guideline</th><th>Scripture</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Pray about the decision</td><td>Philippians 4:6-7</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Let Scripture guide the choice</td><td>Psalm 119:105</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Seek godly counsel</td><td>Proverbs 15:22</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Trust God, not self-understanding alone</td><td>Proverbs 3:5-6</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to contrast Solomon\'s wise request for wisdom with Lot\'s decision based purely on material gain — a common comparison in exam questions on decision-making.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Christian decision-making should be guided by prayer, Scripture, godly counsel, and trust in God, rather than emotion, pressure, or material gain alone.\n</div>\n',
