    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Greed</span> — an excessive, selfish desire for more than one needs, especially wealth or possessions — is repeatedly condemned in Scripture. This topic examines biblical examples of greed and its destructive consequences.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Achan\'s Greed</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">During the conquest of Jericho, Achan coveted and secretly took silver, gold, and a beautiful robe that had been devoted to destruction, disobeying God\'s explicit command. His greed brought Israel\'s defeat at Ai and led to his own death and that of his family (Joshua 7:1, 20-26).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Gehazi\'s Greed</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Gehazi, servant of the prophet Elisha, secretly pursued Naaman after Elisha had refused payment for healing Naaman\'s leprosy, deceitfully requesting silver and clothing for himself. As a result, Elisha pronounced that Naaman\'s leprosy would cling to Gehazi and his descendants forever (2 Kings 5:20-27).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Judas Iscariot\'s Greed</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Judas, one of Jesus\' twelve disciples, was known to steal from the disciples\' common money bag (John 12:6) and ultimately betrayed Jesus for thirty pieces of silver (Matthew 26:14-16). Overcome with remorse afterward, he returned the money and took his own life (Matthew 27:3-5).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Consequences and Lessons of Greed</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">In each case, greed led to deception, betrayal of trust, and severe personal consequences — death, disease, or destruction. The Bible warns, "For the love of money is a root of all kinds of evil" (1 Timothy 6:10), and Jesus taught, "Watch out! Be on your guard against all kinds of greed; life does not consist in an abundance of possessions" (Luke 12:15).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Act of Greed</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Achan</td><td>Took devoted silver, gold, and a robe</td><td>Israel\'s defeat at Ai; his death and his family\'s death</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Gehazi</td><td>Deceitfully collected payment from Naaman</td><td>Struck with leprosy for himself and his descendants</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Judas</td><td>Stole from the money bag; betrayed Jesus for silver</td><td>Remorse and suicide</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember exact figures where relevant — Judas received thirty pieces of silver (Matthew 26:15) — and be able to quote 1 Timothy 6:10 and Luke 12:15 as key verses on greed.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Achan, Gehazi, and Judas each illustrate how greed leads to disobedience, deception, and severe consequences, warning believers to guard their hearts against the love of money and possessions.\n</div>\n',
