    module: 'Nature of God',
    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The Bible consistently portrays God as <span class="learn-keyword">supreme</span> — sovereign over nations, rulers, and all creation. This topic examines biblical evidence of God\'s supremacy and its implications for Christian life.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. God\'s Supremacy Over Nations and Rulers</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nebuchadnezzar, the powerful king of Babylon, was humbled by God after boasting of his own greatness, driven to live like an animal for seven years until he acknowledged, "the Most High rules the kingdom of men, and gives it to whomever He chooses" (Daniel 4:28-37). Similarly, Pharaoh\'s repeated resistance to God\'s demand to free Israel ultimately failed, demonstrating that no earthly ruler can withstand God\'s will (Exodus 5-14).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. God\'s Supremacy Over Creation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Genesis 1 presents God as the sole Creator of the heavens, the earth, and all living things, simply by His spoken word. The Psalms repeatedly celebrate God\'s sovereign control over nature: "The earth is the Lord\'s, and everything in it" (Psalm 24:1), and, "He who sits enthroned above the circle of the earth" (Isaiah 40:22).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. God\'s Supremacy Demonstrated Through Miracles</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">God\'s power over nature is displayed in events like the parting of the Red Sea (Exodus 14), the plagues of Egypt that overpowered Egyptian gods associated with the Nile, sun, and other natural forces (Exodus 7-12), and Jesus calming the storm, prompting His disciples to ask, "What kind of man is this? Even the winds and the waves obey him!" (Matthew 8:23-27).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Lessons for Christians Today</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Recognizing God\'s supremacy calls Christians to humility before Him, confidence that no earthly power can ultimately thwart God\'s purposes, and worship that acknowledges Him alone as sovereign over all creation and history.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Area of Supremacy</th><th>Biblical Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Over rulers/nations</td><td>Nebuchadnezzar\'s humbling (Daniel 4); Pharaoh\'s defeat (Exodus)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Over creation</td><td>Genesis 1 creation account; Psalm 24:1</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Over nature (miracles)</td><td>Red Sea parting; Jesus calming the storm</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that Nebuchadnezzar was humbled for seven years and lived like an animal (eating grass, hair growing like eagle\'s feathers) before acknowledging God\'s supremacy (Daniel 4:33).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> God\'s supremacy over nations, rulers, and all creation teaches Christians to humble themselves before Him and to trust confidently in His ultimate sovereignty over every circumstance.\n</div>\n',
