    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Throughout Israel\'s history, godly kings occasionally led <span class="learn-keyword">religious reforms</span> to remove idolatry and restore true worship of God. This topic examines the reforms of <span class="learn-keyword">Josiah</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">Nehemiah</span> as examples of restoring true worship.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Josiah\'s Religious Reform</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Josiah became king of Judah at just eight years old and, as a young man, began seeking God. During temple repairs, the Book of the Law was rediscovered, and upon hearing it read, Josiah tore his clothes in distress at how far the nation had strayed from God\'s commands (2 Kings 22:8-13). He then led a thorough reform: destroying idols and pagan altars, removing corrupt priests, restoring the Passover celebration, and renewing the covenant with God before the people (2 Kings 23:1-25).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Nehemiah\'s Religious Reform</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">After rebuilding Jerusalem\'s walls, Nehemiah, as governor, worked with Ezra the priest to lead the people in a renewed covenant with God, including public reading and teaching of the Law (Nehemiah 8). Nehemiah also enforced reforms such as stopping trade on the Sabbath, addressing intermarriage with pagan nations that led to idolatry, and ensuring the temple and its services were properly supported (Nehemiah 13:1-31).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Common Features of True Reform</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Both reforms involved returning to Scripture as the standard for right worship, removing practices that compromised loyalty to God, public repentance and covenant renewal, and decisive leadership willing to confront long-standing wrongs.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Importance of True Worship and Reform in Christian Life</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">These reforms teach that true worship must be continually guarded and, when compromised, requires courageous correction. They remind Christians of the ongoing need to examine their faith practices against God\'s word and to repent and realign when they have drifted.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Leader</th><th>Key Reform Action</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Josiah</td><td>Destroyed idols; restored Passover; renewed the covenant after rediscovering the Book of the Law</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Nehemiah</td><td>Renewed covenant reading with Ezra; enforced Sabbath observance; addressed intermarriage; supported temple worship</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember that Josiah\'s reform was triggered by the rediscovery of the Book of the Law during temple repairs — a frequently tested detail (2 Kings 22:8).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Josiah and Nehemiah\'s reforms show that returning to Scripture, decisive leadership, and public renewal of covenant with God are essential elements of restoring true worship after periods of spiritual decline.\n</div>\n',
