    term: 'Second Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  <span class="learn-keyword">Nehemiah</span> stands as a model of deep patriotism and concern for the wellbeing of his nation. This topic examines his example and what it teaches about the responsibilities of Christians toward their nation.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Nehemiah\'s Concern for Jerusalem</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">While serving as cupbearer to King Artaxerxes of Persia, Nehemiah learned that Jerusalem\'s walls lay broken and its gates burned, leaving the city and its people vulnerable and in disgrace. This news deeply grieved him, leading him to weep, mourn, fast, and pray for days (Nehemiah 1:1-4).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Nehemiah\'s Prayer for His Nation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nehemiah\'s prayer combined confession of Israel\'s collective sin, remembrance of God\'s promises, and a specific request for favour before the king (Nehemiah 1:5-11). This model prayer reflects genuine concern that moves from grief to intercession to action.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Nehemiah\'s Action for His Nation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nehemiah requested permission and resources from King Artaxerxes to return to Jerusalem and rebuild its walls (Nehemiah 2:1-8). Despite opposition from Sanballat, Tobiah, and Geshem, he organized the people, and the wall was rebuilt in just fifty-two days (Nehemiah 4; 6:15).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Responsibilities of Christians Toward Their Nation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Nehemiah\'s example teaches believers to genuinely care about their nation\'s condition, to pray earnestly for it, to use their position and resources to contribute to its wellbeing, and to persevere through opposition in pursuing what benefits the wider community. This reflects broader biblical teaching, such as Jeremiah\'s instruction to "seek the peace and prosperity of the city" (Jeremiah 29:7).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Nehemiah\'s Response</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Hearing bad news</td><td>Wept, mourned, fasted, and prayed (Nehemiah 1:4)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Praying</td><td>Confessed sin, recalled God\'s promises, requested favour (Nehemiah 1:5-11)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Acting</td><td>Sought royal permission, organized rebuilding, overcame opposition (Nehemiah 2, 4, 6)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember Nehemiah\'s official position (cupbearer to King Artaxerxes) and the exact number of days it took to complete the wall (fifty-two days, Nehemiah 6:15) — both are frequently tested details.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Nehemiah\'s genuine grief over his nation\'s condition, his earnest prayer, and his decisive action provide a model for Christian patriotism — caring, praying, and working for the wellbeing of one\'s country.\n</div>\n',
