    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Hebrews chapter 11, often called the "faith chapter," presents a roll call of men and women who lived by <span class="learn-keyword">faith in God</span>. This topic explores this chapter\'s examples and the benefits of faith in Christian living.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Definition and Nature of Faith</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Hebrews 11:1 defines faith as "the substance of things hoped for, the evidence of things not seen" — a confident trust in God\'s promises and character even without physical proof. Hebrews 11:6 further teaches that "without faith it is impossible to please God," since anyone who comes to Him must believe He exists and rewards those who seek Him.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Examples of Faith in Hebrews 11</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The chapter lists numerous examples: Abel offered a better sacrifice by faith; Enoch walked with God and did not experience death; Noah built the ark in faith though he had never seen rain; Abraham obeyed God\'s call and offered Isaac by faith; Moses\' parents hid him by faith, and Moses himself chose to suffer with God\'s people rather than enjoy Egypt\'s pleasures; and by faith the Israelites crossed the Red Sea and the walls of Jericho fell (Hebrews 11:4-31).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Faith Amid Suffering and Without Receiving the Promise</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Hebrews 11:13 notes that many of these figures died without receiving what was promised, having only seen it "from afar," yet they remained faithful. Hebrews 11:32-38 also mentions others who suffered greatly — some tortured, others facing mocking, chains, and even death — yet did not renounce their faith.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Benefits of Faith in Christian Living</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Faith pleases God (Hebrews 11:6), provides the confidence to obey God even without full understanding, sustains believers through suffering and uncertainty, and connects believers to God\'s promises even when fulfilment is not immediately visible.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Person</th><th>Act of Faith</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Abel</td><td>Offered a better sacrifice than Cain</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Noah</td><td>Built the ark though he had never seen rain</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Abraham</td><td>Obeyed God\'s call and was willing to offer Isaac</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Moses</td><td>Chose to suffer with God\'s people rather than enjoy Egypt\'s pleasures</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be ready to quote Hebrews 11:1 and 11:6 exactly, and to list at least four to five named examples of faith from the chapter with their specific acts of faith.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Hebrews 11 presents faith as confident trust in God\'s promises, demonstrated through obedience even without seeing the full outcome — a quality essential for pleasing God and sustaining believers through life\'s uncertainties.\n</div>\n',
