    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The Hebrew prophets <span class="learn-keyword">Amos</span>, <span class="learn-keyword">Micah</span>, and <span class="learn-keyword">Isaiah</span> spoke boldly against empty religious ritual that ignored justice and righteousness. This topic examines their teaching on true religion and social justice.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Amos on Justice</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Amos condemned Israel\'s exploitation of the poor — selling the needy for a pair of sandals, trampling the heads of the poor into the dust (Amos 2:6-7) — while they maintained elaborate religious festivals. He declared God\'s rejection of empty worship: "Let justice roll on like a river, righteousness like a never-failing stream" (Amos 5:21-24).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Micah on True Religion</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Micah summarized God\'s true requirement in one of Scripture\'s most quoted verses: "He has shown you, O mortal, what is good. And what does the Lord require of you? To act justly and to love mercy and to walk humbly with your God" (Micah 6:8), directly rejecting the idea that multiplying sacrifices could substitute for justice and mercy.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Isaiah on True Fasting and Worship</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Isaiah rebuked the people for fasting while continuing to oppress workers and quarrel with one another, declaring that the fasting God desires involves loosing the chains of injustice, sharing food with the hungry, providing shelter for the poor, and clothing the naked (Isaiah 58:3-7).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Implications for Christians Today</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">These prophets teach that authentic faith must be expressed in just, compassionate treatment of others, not merely in religious observance. Ritual and worship divorced from justice and mercy are considered offensive to God.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Prophet</th><th>Key Teaching</th><th>Reference</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Amos</td><td>Let justice roll like a river; rejects empty festivals</td><td>Amos 5:21-24</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Micah</td><td>Act justly, love mercy, walk humbly with God</td><td>Micah 6:8</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Isaiah</td><td>True fasting frees the oppressed and feeds the hungry</td><td>Isaiah 58:3-7</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Memorize Micah 6:8 word-for-word — it is one of the most commonly quoted verses in WAEC/NECO CRS examinations on this theme.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Amos, Micah, and Isaiah teach that true religion cannot be separated from justice, mercy, and compassionate care for the poor and oppressed — ritual without righteousness is unacceptable to God.\n</div>\n',
