    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The <span class="learn-keyword">baptism</span> and <span class="learn-keyword">temptation</span> of Jesus mark the beginning of His public ministry. This topic examines both events and their significance and lessons for Christians.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Baptism of Jesus</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Jesus came to John the Baptist at the River Jordan to be baptized. Though John initially objected, feeling unworthy to baptize Jesus, Jesus insisted, saying it was proper "to fulfil all righteousness" (Matthew 3:13-15). As Jesus came up from the water, the heavens opened, the Holy Spirit descended on Him like a dove, and a voice from heaven declared, "This is my beloved Son, in whom I am well pleased" (Matthew 3:16-17).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Significance of Jesus\' Baptism</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Jesus\' baptism identified Him with sinful humanity though He Himself was without sin, publicly launched His ministry, and revealed the Trinity together — the Son being baptized, the Spirit descending, and the Father\'s voice speaking from heaven.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Temptation of Jesus in the Wilderness</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Immediately after His baptism, Jesus was led by the Spirit into the wilderness, where He fasted forty days and nights before being tempted by the devil in three specific ways: to turn stones into bread to satisfy His hunger, to throw Himself from the temple to test God\'s protection, and to worship Satan in exchange for all the kingdoms of the world (Matthew 4:1-11).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. How Jesus Overcame Temptation</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">In each temptation, Jesus responded by quoting Scripture from Deuteronomy, demonstrating the importance of God\'s word in resisting temptation: "Man shall not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God" (Matthew 4:4); "Do not put the Lord your God to the test" (4:7); "Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only" (4:10).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Temptation</th><th>Jesus\' Response (Scripture Quoted)</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Turn stones to bread</td><td>"Man shall not live on bread alone, but on every word that comes from the mouth of God" (Deuteronomy 8:3)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Throw Himself from the temple</td><td>"Do not put the Lord your God to the test" (Deuteronomy 6:16)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Worship Satan for the kingdoms of the world</td><td>"Worship the Lord your God, and serve him only" (Deuteronomy 6:13)</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn all three temptations in order along with Jesus\' exact scriptural response to each — this sequence is a very common exam question format (list-and-match).\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Jesus\' baptism publicly launched His ministry and revealed the Trinity, while His temptation in the wilderness modelled how to resist Satan\'s schemes through knowledge and application of Scripture.\n</div>\n',
