    topic: 'Call of the Disciples',
    topicCode: 'SS2-CRS-17',
    module: 'Life of Christ',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  At the start of His public ministry, Jesus called ordinary men to leave their occupations and follow Him as His <span class="learn-keyword">disciples</span>. This topic examines the call of the twelve, the reasons behind their selection, and the lessons for Christian discipleship.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Calling of the First Disciples</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Walking along the Sea of Galilee, Jesus called Simon Peter and his brother Andrew, who were casting a net into the lake, saying, "Come, follow me, and I will send you out to fish for people" (Matthew 4:18-19). They immediately left their nets and followed Him. Jesus then called James and John, sons of Zebedee, who were mending their nets with their father; they too left immediately, leaving their father and the boat behind (Matthew 4:21-22).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Call of Matthew (Levi)</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Jesus also called Matthew, a tax collector — a profession despised by many Jews for its association with Roman taxation and corruption — while he sat at his tax booth. Matthew immediately got up and followed Jesus, later hosting a dinner where Jesus ate with "tax collectors and sinners" (Matthew 9:9-13).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Twelve Disciples</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">After a night of prayer, Jesus chose twelve from among His followers to be His closest disciples, later called apostles: Simon (Peter), Andrew, James, John, Philip, Bartholomew, Thomas, Matthew, James son of Alphaeus, Thaddaeus, Simon the Zealot, and Judas Iscariot (Luke 6:12-16).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Reasons for Their Call and Lessons for Christians</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Jesus chose ordinary, often unremarkable people — fishermen, a tax collector — rather than the religiously elite, showing that God can use anyone willing to respond in obedience. Their call teaches believers that discipleship requires immediate, wholehearted response and willingness to leave behind former priorities to follow Christ.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Disciple(s)</th><th>Former Occupation</th><th>Response to the Call</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Simon Peter and Andrew</td><td>Fishermen</td><td>Left their nets immediately</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>James and John</td><td>Fishermen</td><td>Left the boat and their father immediately</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Matthew (Levi)</td><td>Tax collector</td><td>Left his tax booth immediately</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Be able to list all twelve disciples by name (Luke 6:12-16) and know each of the first four disciples\' original occupation and family relationships.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Jesus called ordinary people from diverse and even socially despised occupations to be His disciples, and their immediate, wholehearted response models the kind of committed discipleship Jesus still calls believers to today.\n</div>\n',
