    topic: 'The Mission of the Disciples (Luke 10:1-24)',
    topicCode: 'SS2-CRS-18',
    module: 'Life of Christ',
    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  In Luke 10:1-24, Jesus sends out seventy(-two) of His followers on a specific mission, expanding His ministry reach. This topic examines their commissioning, instructions, experiences, and the lessons drawn from their mission.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. The Sending of the Seventy</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Jesus appointed seventy (some manuscripts say seventy-two) others and sent them in pairs ahead of Him into every town and place He intended to visit, to prepare the way for His coming (Luke 10:1). He noted, "The harvest is plentiful, but the workers are few. Ask the Lord of the harvest, therefore, to send out workers into his harvest field" (Luke 10:2).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Jesus\' Instructions for the Mission</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Jesus instructed them to travel light — without purse, bag, or sandals — trusting God\'s provision, to greet no one along the road (emphasizing urgency), to stay in whatever home welcomed them, to eat what was offered, to heal the sick, and to proclaim, "The kingdom of God has come near to you" (Luke 10:3-9). If a town rejected them, they were to wipe the dust off their feet as a testimony against it (Luke 10:10-11).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. The Disciples\' Report and Jesus\' Response</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The seventy returned with joy, reporting, "Lord, even the demons submit to us in your name" (Luke 10:17). Jesus affirmed their authority over evil but redirected their joy: "Do not rejoice that the spirits submit to you, but rejoice that your names are written in heaven" (Luke 10:19-20).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Jesus\' Rejoicing and Teaching</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Jesus rejoiced in the Holy Spirit, thanking the Father for revealing spiritual truths to "little children" rather than "the wise and learned" (Luke 10:21). He then privately told the disciples they were blessed to see and hear what many prophets and kings had longed to see but did not (Luke 10:23-24).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Instruction</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Travel without purse, bag, or sandals</td><td>Trust God\'s provision; urgency of the task</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Stay in one house; eat what is given</td><td>Focus on the mission, not comfort or convenience</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Heal the sick and proclaim the kingdom</td><td>Demonstrate and announce God\'s reign</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Wipe dust off feet if rejected</td><td>A symbolic testimony against unrepentant towns</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Remember Jesus\' key redirection of their joy in Luke 10:20 — rejoicing should be rooted in salvation (\'names written in heaven\'), not merely in spiritual power over demons.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The mission of the seventy shows Jesus expanding His ministry through trained followers, teaching principles of dependent faith, urgency, and rightly-placed joy that remain relevant for Christian mission today.\n</div>\n',
