    term: 'Third Term',
    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  The final hours of Jesus\' earthly life involved a series of unjust <span class="learn-keyword">trials</span> before religious and political authorities, culminating in His <span class="learn-keyword">crucifixion</span>. This topic traces these events and their significance.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Arrest and Trial Before the Jewish Authorities</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">After His betrayal by Judas in the Garden of Gethsemane, Jesus was arrested and brought before Caiaphas the high priest and the Sanhedrin (Jewish ruling council). False witnesses testified against Him, and when Caiaphas asked directly if He was the Christ, the Son of God, Jesus affirmed it. The council condemned Him for blasphemy (Matthew 26:57-68).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. Peter\'s Denial</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">While Jesus was on trial, Peter, who had followed at a distance, denied knowing Jesus three times before a rooster crowed, fulfilling Jesus\' earlier prediction. Realizing what he had done, Peter wept bitterly (Matthew 26:69-75).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Trial Before Pilate</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Since the Jewish council lacked authority to execute, Jesus was brought before Pontius Pilate, the Roman governor. Pilate found no basis for a charge against Him and even sent Him to Herod, who also found nothing deserving of death (Luke 23:1-15). Despite Pilate\'s reluctance, the crowd, incited by the chief priests, demanded Jesus\' crucifixion and the release of Barabbas, a known criminal, instead (Matthew 27:15-26).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Crucifixion and Death of Jesus</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Jesus was mocked, flogged, and forced to carry His cross to Golgotha ("the place of the skull"), where He was crucified between two criminals. He spoke several statements from the cross, including "Father, forgive them, for they do not know what they are doing" (Luke 23:34), and finally, "It is finished" (John 19:30), before dying. At His death, the temple curtain was torn in two, and a Roman centurion declared, "Surely this man was the Son of God!" (Mark 15:38-39).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Key Detail</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Jewish trial</td><td>Before Caiaphas and the Sanhedrin; condemned for blasphemy</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Peter\'s denial</td><td>Denied Jesus three times before the rooster crowed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Roman trial</td><td>Before Pilate, then Herod; Pilate found no fault but yielded to the crowd</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Crucifixion</td><td>At Golgotha, between two criminals; temple curtain torn at His death</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn the sequence of trials in order (Sanhedrin → Pilate → Herod → Pilate again) and be ready to quote at least two of Jesus\' statements from the cross with their references.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Jesus endured unjust trials before both Jewish and Roman authorities and was crucified despite being found without fault, fulfilling His mission of sacrificial death for humanity\'s sin.\n</div>\n',
