    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  Following the outpouring of the Holy Spirit at Pentecost, the early believers formed a distinctive <span class="learn-keyword">fellowship</span> marked by devotion, sharing, and unity. This topic examines the nature and characteristics of this fellowship as recorded in Acts.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Pentecost and the Birth of the Church</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">On the day of Pentecost, the Holy Spirit came upon the disciples with the sound of a rushing wind and tongues of fire, enabling them to speak in other languages (Acts 2:1-4). Peter preached boldly, and about three thousand people were added to the believers that day after responding to his message (Acts 2:14-41).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Marks of Early Church Fellowship</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Acts 2:42-47 describes the believers as devoted to the apostles\' teaching, to fellowship, to the breaking of bread, and to prayer. They shared everything in common, selling possessions and goods to give to anyone in need, met together daily in the temple courts and in homes, and ate together "with glad and sincere hearts," praising God.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Practical Sharing and Generosity</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The believers\' generosity extended to selling property and possessions to meet the needs of the community (Acts 2:45; 4:32-35). Barnabas, for example, sold a field and brought the money to the apostles for distribution (Acts 4:36-37), exemplifying the spirit of voluntary, joyful giving that characterized the community.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Result and Significance of This Fellowship</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">This authentic community life resulted in believers enjoying the favour of all the people, and "the Lord added to their number daily those who were being saved" (Acts 2:47). Their fellowship modelled a radical unity and love that testified powerfully to the truth of the gospel.</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Mark of Fellowship</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Devotion to teaching</td><td>Committed to the apostles\' teaching</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Fellowship</td><td>Meeting together regularly, sharing life</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Breaking of bread</td><td>Shared meals, including communion</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Prayer</td><td>Committed to regular, communal prayer</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Sharing possessions</td><td>Selling goods to meet others\' needs</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Learn Acts 2:42 precisely — the four specific things the early believers "devoted themselves to" is a very commonly tested list.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The early church\'s fellowship, marked by devotion to teaching, prayer, shared meals, and generous sharing of possessions, models authentic Christian community that continues to inspire the church today.\n</div>\n',
