    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  A pivotal development in the early church was the extension of the gospel beyond the Jewish people to the <span class="learn-keyword">Gentiles</span> (non-Jews), guided decisively by the <span class="learn-keyword">Holy Spirit</span>. This topic examines this expansion through the accounts of Peter and Cornelius, and Paul\'s missionary work.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Peter\'s Vision and Cornelius</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Cornelius, a Roman centurion and devout Gentile, received a vision instructing him to send for Peter (Acts 10:1-8). Meanwhile, Peter received a vision of a sheet containing various animals, with a voice telling him, "Do not call anything impure that God has made clean" (Acts 10:9-16), preparing him to overcome his hesitation about associating with Gentiles. Peter then visited Cornelius\'s household, and as he preached, the Holy Spirit fell upon the Gentiles present, just as it had on the Jewish believers at Pentecost (Acts 10:44-48).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Jerusalem Council\'s Decision</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">This inclusion of Gentiles raised significant questions about whether they needed to follow Jewish law (particularly circumcision) to be saved. The apostles and elders met at the Jerusalem Council and, guided by the Holy Spirit, concluded that Gentiles did not need to be circumcised or bound by the full Mosaic law to receive salvation, only to abstain from specific practices that would offend Jewish sensibilities (Acts 15:1-29).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Paul\'s Missionary Journeys to the Gentiles</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">The Holy Spirit specifically set apart Barnabas and Saul (Paul) for missionary work among the Gentiles (Acts 13:1-3). Paul undertook several missionary journeys throughout Asia Minor, Greece, and beyond, planting churches and preaching that salvation through Christ was available to Jew and Gentile alike (Acts 13-20; Galatians 3:28).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. The Significance of This Expansion</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">This development fulfilled Jesus\' commission to make disciples "of all nations" (Matthew 28:19) and confirmed that the gospel was for all humanity, not limited to any single ethnic or religious group, reflecting Paul\'s teaching that "there is neither Jew nor Gentile... for you are all one in Christ Jesus" (Galatians 3:28).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Event</th><th>Significance</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Peter\'s vision and Cornelius\'s conversion</td><td>Confirmed Gentiles could receive the Holy Spirit and salvation directly</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>The Jerusalem Council</td><td>Ruled Gentiles need not follow the full Mosaic law to be saved</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Paul\'s missionary journeys</td><td>Spread the gospel widely among Gentile nations</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Know Cornelius\'s occupation (Roman centurion) and the content of Peter\'s vision (a sheet with animals) — these specific details are often tested directly.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Through Peter\'s encounter with Cornelius, the Jerusalem Council\'s ruling, and Paul\'s missionary journeys, the Holy Spirit guided the early church to extend the gospel fully to the Gentiles, confirming salvation is for all people.\n</div>\n',
