    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  As the gospel spread, the early church faced fierce <span class="learn-keyword">opposition</span> from religious authorities. This first study examines the persecution faced by Peter, John, and Stephen, and how the believers responded.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Peter and John Before the Sanhedrin</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">After healing a lame man at the temple gate and preaching boldly, Peter and John were arrested by the temple authorities and brought before the Sanhedrin, who commanded them to stop speaking in Jesus\' name. Peter and John responded, "Judge for yourselves whether it is right in God\'s sight to obey you rather than God. As for us, we cannot help speaking about what we have seen and heard" (Acts 4:1-20).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Believers\' Prayer for Boldness</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Upon their release, Peter and John reported to the other believers, who together prayed not for safety but for continued boldness to speak God\'s word, and for God to stretch out His hand to heal and perform signs (Acts 4:23-31). The place where they were meeting was shaken, and they were filled with the Holy Spirit and spoke the word boldly.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Stephen\'s Arrest and Defence</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Stephen, one of the seven chosen to serve the early church (Acts 6:1-6), was full of God\'s grace and power and performed great wonders. Opponents from various synagogues could not stand against his wisdom, so they stirred up false witnesses and had him seized and brought before the Sanhedrin on false charges of blasphemy (Acts 6:8-15). Stephen delivered a lengthy defence recounting Israel\'s history and accused his listeners of resisting the Holy Spirit as their ancestors had (Acts 7:1-53).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Stephen\'s Martyrdom</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">Enraged by his speech, the Sanhedrin dragged Stephen out of the city and stoned him to death. As he died, Stephen prayed, "Lord Jesus, receive my spirit," and, echoing Jesus\' own words on the cross, "Lord, do not hold this sin against them" (Acts 7:54-60). A young man named Saul (later Paul) was present, approving of his death (Acts 7:58; 8:1).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Person(s)</th><th>Form of Opposition</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Peter and John</td><td>Arrest, questioning, command to stop preaching</td><td>Refused to stop; prayed for boldness</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Stephen</td><td>False witnesses, false accusation, stoning</td><td>Bold defence; forgiveness and prayer even as he died</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Note that Stephen is regarded as the first Christian martyr, and that Saul (later Paul) was present and approved of his death — an important link to later topics on Saul\'s conversion.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> Peter, John, and Stephen faced serious opposition for proclaiming the gospel, yet responded with boldness, faithfulness, and even forgiveness, modelling how believers should respond to persecution.\n</div>\n',
