    contentHTML:
      '\n<div class="learn-intro">\n  This second study of opposition to the gospel examines the persecution and imprisonment faced by <span class="learn-keyword">Peter</span> and by <span class="learn-keyword">Paul and Silas</span>, and the lessons drawn from how they responded through faith and prayer.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">1. Herod\'s Persecution and Peter\'s Imprisonment</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">King Herod Agrippa I had James, brother of John, killed with the sword, and seeing this pleased the Jewish leaders, he also arrested Peter, intending to bring him before the people after Passover (Acts 12:1-4). Peter was kept under heavy guard, chained between two soldiers in prison.</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">2. The Church\'s Prayer and Peter\'s Miraculous Release</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">While Peter was in prison, the church earnestly prayed for him (Acts 12:5). During the night before his planned trial, an angel appeared, caused Peter\'s chains to fall off, and led him past the guards and through the iron gate, which opened by itself, to freedom. Peter went to the house of Mary, mother of John Mark, where believers were praying, and they were astonished to see him (Acts 12:6-17).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">3. Paul and Silas Imprisoned at Philippi</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">After Paul cast a spirit of divination out of a slave girl, her owners, angry at losing their profit from her fortune-telling, had Paul and Silas seized, beaten, and thrown into prison with their feet fastened in stocks (Acts 16:16-24).</p>\n\n<h3 class="learn-subheading">4. Praying and Singing in Prison, and the Earthquake</h3>\n<p class="learn-p">About midnight, Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns to God while the other prisoners listened. Suddenly, a violent earthquake shook the prison, opening all the doors and loosening everyone\'s chains. When the jailer, believing the prisoners had escaped, was about to kill himself, Paul stopped him, and the jailer, deeply moved, asked, "What must I do to be saved?" He and his household were baptized that very night (Acts 16:25-34).</p>\n\n<div class="learn-table-wrap">\n  <table class="learn-table">\n    <thead><tr><th>Person(s)</th><th>Form of Opposition</th><th>God\'s Response / Outcome</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Peter</td><td>Imprisoned by Herod, heavily guarded</td><td>Miraculously freed by an angel after the church\'s prayer</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Paul and Silas</td><td>Beaten and imprisoned at Philippi</td><td>Earthquake freed them; jailer and household converted and baptized</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Note the specific detail that Paul and Silas were praying and singing hymns (not merely praying) at midnight — this detail is frequently quoted directly in exam questions.\n</div>\n\n<div class="learn-tip-box">\n  <span class="learn-tip-icon">🎯</span>\n  <strong>Summary:</strong> The imprisonments of Peter, and of Paul and Silas, show that even in the face of severe persecution, God can miraculously deliver His people, and their faithful worship even in chains can lead others to salvation.\n</div>\n',
